## 2. `@xyflow/react` — Rendering Nodes & Edges

### What it replaces
- This takes over rendering the nodes and the SVG edges between them — the part that used to be manual `<g transform>` / `<path>` wrangling with D3.
- Under the hood, React Flow itself depends on `d3-zoom` for zoom, pan, and drag interactions with the canvas — so the pan/zoom concepts from earlier notes are still relevant, just handled internally by the library instead of wired up by hand.

### Install / Import

```js
npm install @xyflow/react
```

```js
import { ReactFlow } from '@xyflow/react';
```

### Core component

```jsx
import { ReactFlow } from '@xyflow/react'

export default function Flow() {
  return <ReactFlow nodes={...} edges={...} onNodesChange={...} ... />
}
```

- Takes `nodes` and `edges` arrays as its main data props, plus many optional props.
- **Warning**: event handlers passed as props should be defined outside the component or wrapped in `useCallback` — otherwise React Flow can enter an infinite re-render loop.

### Built-in edge types
- Four built-in edge types: `"default"` (bezier), `"straight"`, `"step"`, `"smoothstep"`.
- If you don't set the `type` property on an edge, it falls back to `"default"` (bezier curve).
- `"step"` / `"smoothstep"` produce **orthogonal (right-angled)** connectors — directly relevant if the assignment spec calls for orthogonal edge routing rather than curves.

### Custom edges
- You can build a custom edge component using the `BaseEdge` helper component plus one of the path-generator utilities:
  - `getStraightPath()`
  - `getSmoothStepPath()` — for step/orthogonal-style paths
  - `getBezierPath()` / `getSimpleBezierPath()`

```jsx
import { BaseEdge, type EdgeProps, type Edge, getSmoothStepPath } from '@xyflow/react';

type CustomEdge = Edge<{ value: number }, 'custom'>;

export default function CustomEdge({
  id, sourceX, sourceY, sourcePosition,
  targetX, targetY, targetPosition,
}: EdgeProps<CustomEdge>) {
  const [edgePath] = getSmoothStepPath({
    sourceX, sourceY, sourcePosition,
    targetX, targetY, targetPosition,
  });
  return <BaseEdge id={id} path={edgePath} />;
}
```

- `getSmoothStepPath()` returns a tuple: `[path, labelX, labelY, offsetX, offsetY]`.
  - `path` — the SVG path string to render.
  - `labelX, labelY` — center position of the path (useful for placing a label).
  - `offsetX, offsetY` — offset between the source handle and the label position.
  - To get sharp corners instead of rounded ones (i.e. a true "step" look), pass `borderRadius: 0` into `getSmoothStepPath()`.

### Custom nodes & Handles
- Custom nodes use the `Handle` component to define connection points:

```jsx
import { Position, Handle } from '@xyflow/react';

export function CustomNode() {
  return (
    <div className="custom-node">
      <div>Custom Node Content</div>
      <Handle type="source" position={Position.Top} />
      <Handle type="target" position={Position.Bottom} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

- If a custom node needs multiple source/target handles, each handle needs a unique `id` so React Flow can tell them apart when connecting edges.

### Useful hooks
- `useEdges()` — returns the current array of edges; re-renders the component whenever any edge changes.
- Relying on `useEdges()` (or similar hooks) unnecessarily can cause performance issues, since the component re-renders on every edge change — prefer more targeted hooks/selectors where possible.

### Registering custom types
- Custom node/edge components get mapped to a `type` string via `nodeTypes` / `edgeTypes` objects passed to `ReactFlow`, so a node/edge's `type` property determines which custom component renders it.